\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Berggren tree exhibits the primitive Pythagorean triples as a perfectly regular ternary tree
rooted at $(3,4,5)$, generated by three integer matrices. We identify the mechanism behind this
classical result as reflection in the all-ones vector inside the integral Lorentz form of
signature $(n,1)$, and determine exactly how far it generalises to primitive Pythagorean
$n$-tuples, i.e.\ integral solutions of $x_1^2+\dots+x_n^2=y^2$. Four results delimit the theory.
\emph{Integrality dichotomy}: the all-ones reflection subtracts
$2\bigl(\sum_i\varepsilon_ix_i-y\bigr)/(n-1)$ from every coordinate, hence is integral precisely
when $n-1\mid 2$, i.e.\ for $n=2$ (triples) and $n=3$ (quadruples) only. \emph{Connectivity}: in
dimension three the group generated by the all-ones reflection, one sign change and the
transpositions of the space coordinates acts transitively on the primitive Pythagorean quadruples
of the positive cone, with root $(1,0,0,1)$; the Lorentz form and the content are invariants that
separate this orbit from the imprimitive ones. \emph{Failure of the tree property}: a primitive
triple admits exactly one descending sign pattern, whence constant ternary branching, while a
quadruple admits at most two, the second existing exactly when the harmonic inequality
$\tfrac1b+\tfrac1c<\tfrac1a$ holds; explicit infinite families show that the branching number takes
both values $6$ and $7$ infinitely often, and the equality case $\tfrac1b+\tfrac1c=\tfrac1a$ yields
height-preserving edges, realised at $(1,2,2,3)$ and provably impossible for triples. Nevertheless
the all-plus reflection is a well-founded canonical parent map, so the quadruple graph carries a
canonical spanning tree. \emph{Growth}: one reflection multiplies the height by at most
$\rho_n=(\sqrt n+1)/(\sqrt n-1)$, sharply, and $\rho_n$ is a root of
$(n-1)X^2-2(n+1)X+(n-1)$; thus $\rho_2=3+2\sqrt2=(1+\sqrt2)^2$, the square of the silver ratio, and
$\rho_3=2+\sqrt3$, a root of $X^2-4X+1$.
\end{abstract}

